    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The two sides disagreed about where the border lay: the Japanese held that it was the Khalkhin Gol, the Mongolians and Soviets that it ran 16 kilometres to the east, just east of Nomonhan.</li><li>Zhukov's offensive of 20 August pinned the Japanese in the centre and swept armoured units round both flanks, trapping the Japanese 23rd Infantry Division when the wings linked up on 25 August.</li><li>Japanese casualty figures range from about 19,714 in the Sixth Army's medical data to 35,000 to 36,000 reported by a minister, and the Soviet claims for them rose from 29,085 to 61,000.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Japanese record the fighting as the Nomonhan Incident, after Nomonhan Burd Obo, yet it involved aircraft, tanks and tens of thousands of troops, and it ended only with a cease-fire.</p></aside>
    </section>

