    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> sign a Mutual Assistance Pact, allowing Soviet troops into Mongolia</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1939</span><span class="rf-text">A Mongolian cavalry unit is driven back across the Khalkhin Gol by Manchukoan cavalry</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late May 1939</span><span class="rf-text">Soviet and Mongolian forces surround and destroy the Azuma force</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early July 1939</span><span class="rf-text">The Japanese attack in two prongs, but both are thrown back by 9 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late July 1939</span><span class="rf-text">A second large Japanese assault on the Kawatama Bridge is called off on 25 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1939</span><span class="rf-text">Zhukov opens his offensive with artillery and 557 aircraft</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late August 1939</span><span class="rf-text">The Soviet wings link up near Nomonhan and trap the Japanese 23rd Infantry Division</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1939</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> agree a cease-fire</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battles-of-khalkhin-gol" data-v="73eef2dc14" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Soviet attack opens with guns and aircraft</b> The Soviet and Mongolian force stood west of the Khalkhin Gol with armour on both wings. The Japanese 23rd Infantry Division held the east bank. Artillery and aircraft opened the attack.</li><li><b>The Soviet centre crosses and pins the Japanese</b> Three infantry divisions and a tank brigade crossed the river and attacked the east bank, supported by the guns. The Japanese were held in place while the armour on the wings prepared to move.</li><li><b>The armour sweeps round both flanks</b> Soviet armoured units swept around the flanks of the Japanese and came at them from the rear. Each wing moved round its own side of the Japanese line while the centre held them in front.</li><li><b>The wings meet at Nomonhan</b> The two Soviet wings joined near Nomonhan, which closed a ring behind the Japanese 23rd Infantry Division and trapped it.</li><li><b>The relief and the break-out fail</b> A Japanese counterattack to relieve the 23rd Division failed on 26 August. On 27 August the division tried to break out of the encirclement and also failed.</li><li><b>The encircled force is hit again and broken up</b> The surrounded Japanese would not surrender and were hit again with artillery and air attacks. By 31 August the Japanese on the Mongolian side of the border were destroyed.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact.jpg" alt="Anti-Comintern Pact" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact">Anti-Comintern Pact</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anti-Comintern_Pact_signing_1936.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Japanese occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> turned its military interests to the Soviet territories that bordered the area. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the People's Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> signed a Mutual Assistance Pact in March 1936, allowing Soviet troops into Mongolia, and in the same year Japan signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact">Anti-Comintern Pact</a>. After Japan's full invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in July 1937, the Soviet Union sent the 57th Special Corps under Ivan Konev to Mongolia. Genrikh Lyushkov, a Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> major general, defected to Japan for fear of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, taking secret documents that allegedly revealed the dire situation of the Soviet army in the Far East. The first major Soviet-Japanese border incident, the Battle of Lake Khasan, came in 1938 in Primorye.</p><p>In 1939 Manchuria was a puppet state of Japan known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>, and Mongolia a communist state allied with the Soviet Union. The Japanese maintained that the border between them was the Khalkhin Gol, which flows into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-buir">Lake Buir</a>. The Mongolians and their Soviet allies maintained that it ran 16 kilometres east of the river, just east of Nomonhan. The principal occupying army of Manchukuo, Japan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a>, had instructions from the Japanese Cabinet to strengthen and fortify Manchukuo's borders. Stationed in Manchuria for a long time, it had become largely autonomous and tended to act without approval from the Japanese government, or even against its direction.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fighting began on 11 May 1939, when a Mongolian cavalry unit of 70 to 90 men entered the disputed area in search of grazing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> cavalry drove it back across the river. Both sides then increased their forces. On 5 June Zhukov, the new corps commander, arrived with more motorized and armoured forces, accompanied by the air commander Yakov Smushkevich; Zhamyangiyn Lhagvasuren of the Mongolian army became his deputy. Zhukov's army was 748 kilometres from its base of supply and he assembled 2,600 trucks to feed it. In early July the Japanese had only 600 trucks, rising to 1,000 by the end of the month, of which only 75% were operable.</p><p>For their assault in July the Japanese used three regiments and part of a fourth under Colonel Shinichiro Sumi, to cross the river and take Baintsagan Hill, and the 1st Tank Corps under Lieutenant General Yasuoka Masaomi, made up of the 3rd and 4th Tank Regiments with infantry, engineers and artillery, to attack the east bank.</p><p>By 20 August Zhukov, using at least 4,000 trucks from the base at Chita, 600 kilometres away, had three rifle divisions, two tank divisions and two more tank <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> (around 498 BT-5 and BT-7 tanks), two motorized infantry divisions and over 550 fighters and bombers. The Mongolians committed two cavalry divisions. At the point of contact the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> had only the 23rd Infantry Division, equal with its attached forces to two light infantry divisions, with its headquarters at Hailar, over 150 kilometres from the fighting. Japanese intelligence tracked the build-up but did not provoke an appropriate response from below.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 14 May Lieutenant Colonel Yaozo Azuma led the reconnaissance regiment of the 23rd Infantry Division into the area and the Mongolians withdrew, but when they and the Soviets returned and Azuma's force moved to evict them, they surrounded it on 28 May and destroyed it. It lost eight officers and 97 men killed and one officer and 33 men wounded, 63% total casualties. On 27 June the Japanese Army Air Force's 2nd Air <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> struck the Soviet airbase at Tamsak-Bulak. The strike, ordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> without permission from army headquarters in Tokyo, succeeded, but Tokyo then forbade any more.</p><p>The July assault began with the northern force crossing the Khalkhin Gol and taking Baintsagan Hill. Zhukov counterattacked with 450 tanks and armoured cars, which, though unsupported by infantry, attacked on three sides and nearly encircled the Japanese, who had only one pontoon bridge for supplies and recrossed the river on 5 July. The Yasuoka Detachment attacked on the night of 2 July, lost over half its armour without breaking through, was thrown back on 9 July, and was dissolved. A second assault on 23 July, backed by an artillery barrage that used more than half the ammunition stores, failed and was abandoned on 25 July.</p><p>At 05:45 on 20 August Soviet artillery and 557 aircraft struck the Japanese, and about 50,000 Soviet and Mongolian soldiers attacked the east bank. With the Japanese pinned down by the centre, armoured units swept round the flanks and attacked from the rear in a classic double envelopment. The wings linked up at Nomonhan on 25 August, trapping the 23rd Division. A Japanese counterattack on 26 August failed, as did a breakout on 27 August, and by 31 August the Japanese forces on the Mongolian side were destroyed.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battles of Khalkhin Gol&#8221; cites where it covers June: Escalation, July: Japanese assault, August: Soviet counterattack, Aircraft ordnance expenditures:</p><ul><li>Alvin D. Coox, Nomonhan: Japan Against Russia, 1939 (Stanford University Press, 1985)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> agreed to a cease-fire on 15 September, which took effect at 1:10 pm the next day. Japanese military records reported approximately 20,000 battle and non-battle casualties, 162 aircraft lost in combat and 42 tanks disabled, of which 29 were repaired. Roughly 500 to 600 Japanese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> were taken prisoner; because military doctrine prohibited surrender, most were listed as killed in action for the benefit of their families.</p><p>Zhukov reported Soviet casualties in the offensive from 20 August as 9,284, among them 1,701 dead and missing and 7,583 wounded. For the whole campaign Soviet equipment losses were 253 tanks, 250 aircraft, 96 artillery pieces and 133 armoured cars. Mongolian casualties were 556 to 990, including 280 killed and 710 wounded.</p><p>Zhukov won the first of his four Hero of the Soviet Union awards, as did Grigori Shtern and Yakov Smushkevich, who were both executed in the 1941 purges. Zhukov was promoted and moved west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> district. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> earned the displeasure of officials in Tokyo, less for its defeat than because it had started and escalated the fighting without government authorization. The Soviet Union and Japan signed the Soviet-Japanese Neutrality Pact in April 1941.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The casualty figures are the most disputed part of the record. Japanese military records give about 20,000 casualties. The Sixth Army's medical data give 7,696 killed, 8,647 wounded, 1,021 missing and 2,350 sick, a total of 19,714, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> headquarters gave 8,629 killed and 9,087 injured, excluding sick. In November 1939 the American consulate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> claimed to hold authoritative information that casualties reached 30,000, and in October the Agricultural Minister Yukio Sakurauchi reported 35,000 to 36,000 out of 40,000 participants. A monument of September 1942 attributed 9,471 Japanese and 202 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> to the campaign.</p><p>The Soviets first claimed to have inflicted 29,085 casualties, then 52,000 to 55,000, then 61,000 in their official histories. For their own losses, Grigoriy Krivosheev cited 7,974 killed and 15,251 wounded in 1993, and in 2001 gave revised figures of 9,703 dead and missing, 15,251 wounded and 701 sick, a total of 25,655. Boris Sokolov cites a higher number of sick, 2,225, writing that the lower figure reflects only patients treated in the Trans-Baikal Military District.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan.jpg" alt="Khorloogiin Choibalsan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan">Khorloogiin Choibalsan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horloogiyn_Choybalsan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The victory is credited with altering the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Together with Chinese resistance and the German-Soviet non-aggression pact, which removed the basis of the army's policy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the defeat moved the Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> away from the north-strike policy favoured by the Army, which wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a> for its resources. Support shifted to the south-strike policy favoured by the Navy, aimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. Masanobu Tsuji, who had helped instigate the incident, later wrote that Soviet firepower at Nomonhan convinced him not to attack the Soviet Union in 1941. On 24 June 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s leaders resolved not to intervene in the German-Soviet war for the time being, and Japan attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> on 7 December.</p><p>Zhukov used the experience at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in December 1941, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> in Operation Uranus a year later, using a technique very similar to Khalkhin Gol. The Japanese increased tank production from 500 annually to 1,200 in 1939 and introduced the Type 1 47 mm anti-tank gun. After the war the Soviet Union charged fourteen Japanese at the Tokyo tribunal over the fighting, and Kenji Doihara, Hiranuma Kiichirō and Seishirō Itagaki were convicted on these charges. The Mongolian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan">Choibalsan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a> have museums to Zhukov and the battle, and the anniversaries have been marked with the presidents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battles of Khalkhin Gol&#8221; cites where it covers Soviet assessment, Japanese assessment and reforms:</p><ul><li>Alvin D. Coox, Nomonhan: Japan Against Russia, 1939 (Stanford University Press, 1985)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In 1939 Soviet and Mongolian forces under Zhukov fought Japanese and Manchukoan forces over the Mongolia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a> border. His offensive of 20 August trapped the Japanese 23rd Division, and a cease-fire followed on 15 September. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> afterwards turned its strategy south.</em></p>

